# Execution log: Phase 19 Linux stack-capture CI correction

- Session date: 2026-10-26 UTC
- Candidate under initial remote CI: `187b0427263c476ddee7808d67b7aa79e9ddb7f8`
- Stress implementation: `5e1c80b1716936a04f319832adc9d382adc2b500`
- Initial aggregate evidence commit: `187b0427`
- Java oracle: Apache RocketMQ 5.5.1 source
  `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`, read-only.
- Scope: diagnose the parallel Linux CI failure, improve the safe signal
  fallback pool, and align CI's workspace test execution with the serial mode
  required by the complete local aggregate. No plan files were edited.

## Remote CI diagnostic

- Pushed commit `187b0427` triggered GitHub Actions run
  `40266865631` at
  `https://github.com/triotous/rocketmq-classic/actions/runs/40266865631`.
- The `windows-stack-runtime` job passed. The standard Linux job passed format
  and strict Clippy, then failed the default parallel
  `cargo test --workspace --locked` command. The failing test was
  `stack::tests::concurrent_jstack_captures_are_serialized_without_losing_threads`;
  the captured panic reported that no unused, unblocked default-ignore signal
  was available. The required Docker Phase 19 matrix remained in progress at
  this log's initial write.
- The signal choice is process-wide and depends on every live thread's blocked
  mask. Four signals whose default action is ignore are now considered, and the
  Linux unit tests cover selection of the fourth candidate. When another test
  process thread blocks all four or installs handlers for them, the stack
  implementation's existing current-thread fallback remains appropriate.

## Corrective change and checks

- `.github/workflows/ci.yml` now runs both default and all-feature workspace
  test suites with `-- --test-threads=1`, matching the existing
  `integration-tests/run-local.sh` command and keeping process-global signal
  tests isolated from unrelated concurrently running test threads.
- A Linux ARM64 container using Rust 1.85.0 ran the consumer library suite
  without serialization once: 275 tests passed, then a repeat reproduced the
  signal-mask limitation in `jstack_includes_current_and_other_named_threads`.
  This confirmed the process-wide concurrency issue and justified serial test
  execution rather than weakening either stack-capture assertion.
- The same Linux container then ran the consumer suite with
  `-- --test-threads=1`: all 275 tests passed in 77 seconds. Strict Clippy for
  the consumer library passed in 27.52 seconds. `cargo fmt --all -- --check`
  and `git diff --check` passed on the worktree.
- The remote workflow for the corrected candidate and its Docker matrix remain
  pending; the initial workflow does not validate the correction.

